<header><p class="st-kicker">kingdom · Caucasus</p><h1 class="st-title">Kingdom of Kakheti-Hereti</h1><p class="st-meta">1010 CE – 1104 CE (approx.) · capital Telavi</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">An eastern Georgian kingdom that resisted absorption into the united Georgian monarchy for nearly a century before David the Builder conquered it.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Around 1010 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kvirike-iii-of-kakheti">Kvirike III</a> became chorepiscopus of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/principality-of-kakheti">Principality of Kakheti</a>, which had already absorbed the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/principality-of-hereti">Principality of Hereti</a>, and later took the title king of the Kakhs and Rans. His realm, centred on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/telavi">Telavi</a>, was overrun by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bagrat-iii-of-georgia">Bagrat III</a>, who was uniting the rest of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/georgia">Georgia</a>, but regained its independence after Bagrat's death in 1014.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kvirike-iii-of-kakheti">Kvirike III</a>, called the Great, was a strong ruler and builder who allied with neighbours against the Georgian kings. The kingdom lived from the vineyards and pastures of the Alazani valley and controlled routes towards <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/shirvanshah-state">Shirvan</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aghsartan-i-of-kakheti">Aghsartan I</a> submitted to the Seljuk sultan <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alp-arslan">Alp Arslan</a> in 1068 and outwardly converted to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a> to keep his throne.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>Seljuk overlordship weakened the kingdom, and in 1104 nobles of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/principality-of-hereti">Hereti</a> handed King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aghsartan-ii-of-kakheti">Aghsartan II</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/david-iv-of-georgia">David IV</a>, who annexed it to the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/kingdom-of-georgia">Kingdom of Georgia</a>. <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/principality-of-kakheti">Kakheti</a> re-emerged as a separate kingdom in 1465.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/kingdom-of-kakheti-hereti.jpg" alt="Kingdom of Kakheti-Hereti" width="320" height="248"><figcaption>Image: Don-kun · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Caucasus_1000_map_alt_de.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1010 CE – 1104 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/11th-century">11th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/12th-century">12th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>kingdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Telavi</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Kvirike III, Aghsartan I, Aghsartan II</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Georgian Orthodox Christianity</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Georgian</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/georgia">Georgia</a>, <a href="/countries/azerbaijan">Azerbaijan</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Kingdom of the Kakhs and Rans, Kingdom of Kakheti (early)</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1057&amp;focus=kingdom-of-kakheti-hereti" aria-label="Kingdom of Kakheti-Hereti on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5786 685 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Kingdom of Kakheti-Hereti lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M6106 941L6106 941M6106 941L6153 964M6106 941L6481 1127M6106 941L6036 930"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M6106 941h0M6153 964h0M6481 1127h0M6036 930h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M6106 941h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M6106 941h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Telavi. <a href="/atlas?year=1057&amp;focus=kingdom-of-kakheti-hereti">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
